1
00:00:00,725 --> 00:00:03,535
Thirteen metres from home, carrying 21 samples,

2
00:00:03,575 --> 00:00:05,660
this rover's battery runs flat.

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:08,260
Its autopilot was written by Claude Opus.

4
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:10,475
Score for this site: zero.

5
00:00:12,175 --> 00:00:13,872
This is Lightlag Traverse:

6
00:00:13,912 --> 00:00:16,348
one day of driving on Mars-like ground.

7
00:00:16,388 --> 00:00:18,460
Every command lands three ticks late,

8
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:21,375
and every position fix is three ticks old.

9
00:00:22,075 --> 00:00:23,522
The ground fights back.

10
00:00:23,562 --> 00:00:25,510
Wheels slip on sand and slopes,

11
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:28,385
so a rover is rarely where its program thinks.

12
00:00:28,425 --> 00:00:31,148
3 cm too far from a target is a miss;

13
00:00:31,188 --> 00:00:35,400
ground too steep trips a safety stop that wipes every queued command.

14
00:00:36,125 --> 00:00:38,010
And the only fuel is the sun.

15
00:00:38,050 --> 00:00:41,635
At 9% battery, our reference stops and waits for it.

16
00:00:41,675 --> 00:00:45,160
Four ticks later, Claude's rover drives its battery to zero,

17
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:46,910
and the site scores nothing.

18
00:00:46,950 --> 00:00:49,325
Six ticks of waiting would have saved it.

19
00:00:50,783 --> 00:00:54,243
In its own report, Claude says its planner only counted time,

20
00:00:54,283 --> 00:00:57,706
and that all three sites were played before it checked one result.

21
00:00:57,746 --> 00:00:59,418
Claude doesn't fail at coding.

22
00:00:59,458 --> 00:01:01,508
It fails at planning the day.

23
00:01:02,825 --> 00:01:05,360
The reference is a planner, not a model.

24
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:07,248
It tracks the rover through the delay,

25
00:01:07,288 --> 00:01:09,085
learns how each surface slips,

26
00:01:09,125 --> 00:01:12,050
and prices every leg of its route against the sun.

27
00:01:13,883 --> 00:01:16,943
Through Harbor, the reference scores 0.48.

28
00:01:16,983 --> 00:01:21,418
Seven Claude runs, from Haiku to Opus, all finish below 0.3,

29
00:01:21,458 --> 00:01:22,980
and three lost a rover.

30
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:24,883
Doing nothing scores zero.

31
00:01:26,125 --> 00:01:30,435
On Modal, six of Claude's programs replayed 300 fresh worlds each.

32
00:01:30,475 --> 00:01:35,298
The reference scored higher 99 times in 100 and never lost a rover.

33
00:01:35,338 --> 00:01:37,022
Claude's few wins were narrow,

34
00:01:37,062 --> 00:01:40,825
mostly from programs that ran flat on up to half their worlds.

35
00:01:42,025 --> 00:01:44,560
A policy trained there from scratch learns too:

36
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:49,000
in development it reaches 0.38, still short of the reference.

37
00:01:50,350 --> 00:01:51,985
One command reruns it all,

38
00:01:52,025 --> 00:01:54,985
and a live demo on Modal plays any world you pick.

39
00:01:55,025 --> 00:01:56,750
Can your agent make it home?

